WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.230
A hidden pattern is popping up
in seemingly unrelated places,

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:07.290
from a bus system in
Mexico to chicken eyes

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:09.540
to number theory
and quantum physics.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.240
This phenomenon,
known as universality,

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.060
continues to surprise
mathematicians and reveal

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.355
a deeper understanding
of our world.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:27.190
Imagine arriving at an empty
bus stop in New York City.

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:29.320
The last bus must
have just left.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:33.010
But the sign says a bus comes
every 10 minutes on average.

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:35.290
What's the probability
that the next bus will

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:37.960
arrive within five minutes?

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.360
The probability of a
random event happening

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.720
within some interval, like a
bus coming within the next five

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:48.290
minutes, is given by a curve
called a Poisson distribution.

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:51.580
But what if bus arrival
times are not independent?

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:54.610
In the 1970s in
Cuernavaca, Mexico,

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:58.200
bus drivers would hire spies
to sit along their route.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.090
And drivers either speed
up or wait at a stop

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:05.530
depending on how long ago their
spy said the previous bus let.

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:09.100
This spaced out the buses
and maximized their profits.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.380
In that case, the
spacing between buses

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:15.370
is defined by a very different
probability distribution.

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:18.280
The Cuernavaca bus system,
the Riemann zeta function

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:20.980
related to prime
numbers, chicken retinas,

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.440
and atomic nuclei
are all examples

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:26.270
of complex correlated systems.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:29.190
The components of these
systems aren't independent.

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:31.420
They interact and
repel one another.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.960
And this leads to a
statistical distribution

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.880
in between randomness and order.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.670
The same distribution
or pattern arises,

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:41.980
even though the components
of these various systems

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:43.640
are very different.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.610
They are said to
exhibit universality.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:49.530
Mathematicians
model these complex

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:52.390
correlated systems
using random matrices.

00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:55.140
The numbers in random
matrices are drawn randomly

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.390
from probability distributions.

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:59.070
The matrix might
be randomly filled

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:01.950
with zeros and ones
or any set of numbers,

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:04.350
like the integers
between 1 and 100.

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:07.680
You can characterize a
matrix by its eigenvalues,

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.780
a series of numbers
that can be calculated

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:11.940
by multiplying
components of the matrix

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:14.700
together in a certain way.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.500
Eigenvalues of certain
random matrices

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.390
are always spaced
along a number line

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:22.050
in a characteristic pattern,
with consecutive eigenvalues

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:25.620
never too close together
or too far apart.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.960
The same pattern of
eigenvalue spacings

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:32.250
arises no matter how you fill
the matrix with random numbers.

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:35.280
If you plot the distance
between consecutive eigenvalues

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:38.160
on the x-axis and the
probability of getting

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.040
a particular spacing
on the y-axis,

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.400
the familiar lopsided
curve begins to appear.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.860
Researchers are still
looking for a general answer

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.410
to where this universal
pattern comes from,

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:52.120
but clues continue to emerge.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:54.840
The idea of random
matrix universality

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.780
goes back to Eugene Wigner,
a Nobel Prize winning

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:01.200
theoretical physicist who
worked on the Manhattan Project.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.450
Wigner was attempting
to calculate the energy

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:05.730
levels of a uranium
nucleus, which

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:08.400
has more than 200
protons and neutrons

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.730
that can arrange themselves in
all different configurations.

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:14.070
The associated energy
levels of the system

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:17.110
were far too complex
to calculate.

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:20.040
Wigner used random
matrices instead

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:23.910
and plotted the statistical
distribution of eigenvalues.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:26.100
He found that the
spacing of these numbers

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.890
matched the spacing of
energy levels of uranium

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:32.620
and other heavy atomic nuclei.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:34.380
Two decades later,
the pattern was

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.380
seen in gaps between
consecutive numbers called zeros

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:39.330
of the Riemann zeta function.

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:41.100
These zeroes are
thought to control how

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.380
prime numbers are distributed.

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:45.390
Since then, the
pattern has been seen

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:48.090
in many different settings,
like in human bones

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:50.370
and social networks.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:53.850
Just recently, it showed up
in yet another unlikely place,

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:55.650
the eyes of chickens.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:59.520
It was the first instance of
the pattern seen in biology.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:02.250
While the number line exhibits
a pattern of universality

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:05.040
in one dimension, the
chicken retina cells

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:08.270
reveals it in two dimensions.

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:11.310
The color-sensitive cone
cells of a chicken's retina

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:14.940
seem haphazardly distributed,
but with a remarkably uniform

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:16.649
density.

00:04:16.649 --> 00:04:19.260
Looking closer, the cells
appear to be surrounded

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:21.600
by what's called an
exclusion region,

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:24.510
a space where cones with
different color sensitivity can

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:29.360
be found, but cones of
the same kind can cannot.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:31.830
Just how these cones
cells create the exclusion

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:33.710
zones remains a
mystery, but it's

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:37.100
similar to the repulsion between
consecutive random matrix

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:39.220
eigenvalues on the number line.

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:41.610
Researchers say
we're just at the tip

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:45.840
of the iceberg in understanding
universality in math, physics,

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:48.110
and even biology.

